# Loading — sections and tiles

Two distinct UX modes; do not mix them.

## Initial load (no cached data for that surface)

- Use the design system **regional loader** (`LoadingState` with `size="region"`, or equivalent documented wait mark for large viewports when already standardized on that page).
- **Center** it in the section or tile body.
- Replace section/tile **content** until the first successful fetch (or explicit error/empty).

## Background refresh (data already shown; query refetch / invalidation)

- **Do not** swap the section body for a regional loader (no layout shift, no hiding metrics/CTAs).
- Replace only the **section marker** in the header: numbered chip, rail icon, or tile icon — with a **`ds-spinner` in the same footprint** (fixed width/height matching the chip or icon slot).
- Keep **`aria-busy`** on the section/tile (or strip) and a **visually hidden** `role="status"` message for screen readers.
- **Mutations** (button submit): spinner on the control (`PendingLabel` / inline `ds-spinner`), not a full-section loader, unless the entire section truly has no stable content yet.

## Reference implementation

- Operational strip sections: `StripSection` + `titleChipRefreshing` (`structureOperationalStripSection.tsx`), driven by hub background refetch in `StructurePhaseFiche`.
- Schematic tile: `StructureSchematicViewport` — initial load and schematic-query refetch both show centered `WaitMark` in the viewport (schematic hidden until fetch settles).

## Anti-patterns

- Per-column “updating…” text rows that grow layout.
- Full-section `LoadingState` during hub refetch when children can stay visible.
- Using `PendingLabel` in the tile body for **server refetch** (reserve for **in-flight mutations**).
